<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">We Shall Overcome</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">hymn roots <a href="/years/1901">1901</a>; sung in the Charleston strike <a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a>; first printed 1947 or 1948; anthem of the civil rights movement from <a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The roots of &quot;We Shall Overcome&quot; are unclear and many. It was long thought to descend from &quot;I'll Overcome Some Day&quot;, a hymn by the Reverend Charles Albert Tindley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, a Methodist Episcopal minister, first published in 1901, written in an idiom rooted in African American folk traditions for congregations many of whom had been freed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> a generation before.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1965 Aboriginal and white students led by Charles Perkins toured New South Wales on a “Freedom Ride” against segregation, and in 1968 and 1969 marchers of the Northern Ireland Civil Rights Association, which modelled itself on the American movement, sang “<b>We Shall Overcome</b>”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the civil rights movement of the 1950s and 1960s, African Americans criticised &quot;Dixie&quot; as a racist relic of the Confederacy and a reminder of white domination and segregation, all the more when white opponents of civil rights sang it in answer to &quot;<b>We Shall Overcome</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dixie">Dixie</a></li><li><blockquote>The Weavers sang it at their Carnegie Hall reunion concert in May 1963, and shortly afterwards Seeger put it on his live album <b>We Shall Overcome</b>, a recording AllMusic's Stewart Mason calls the definitive version.</blockquote><a href="/songs/guantanamera">Guantanamera</a></li><li><blockquote>The activist Duma Ndlovu compared its influence to that of the American protest song &quot;<b>We Shall Overcome</b>&quot;, and Pete Seeger already had an adaptation of it in his repertoire in the 1950s and 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/songs/senzeni-na">Senzeni Na?</a></li><li><blockquote>The song was featured in the musical King Kong (1959), whose London production followed in 1961, and Pete Seeger recorded it on his album <b>We Shall Overcome</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/shosholoza">Shosholoza</a></li><li><blockquote>The roots of &quot;<b>We Shall Overcome</b>&quot; are unclear and many.</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-shall-overcome">We Shall Overcome</a></li></ul></section>
